<description>
# A Power Meter on the lights of the microscope

The demo of the **Power Meter** for a DC supply (`docs/POWER_METER.md`): a 12 V battery feeding two white LEDs through 330 Ω each, with the meter in the battery's + lead, its red lead toward the battery.

Run: both LEDs light, 26.7 mA each, and the meter reads **640.9 mW**, 12.00 V, 53.41 mA, power factor **1.000** (a battery and resistors: voltage and current in phase and steady).

An ammeter in this lead would read 53.4 mA, the same as on a 6 V pack at the same light: the meter shows the other factor of the energy, the voltage, and their product.

| Part | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `BAT` | 12 V | The microscope's plan: 12 V from a battery or an adapter. |
| `PM1` | Power Meter, `Reading` Instant | The new part, in the + lead. |
| `R1`, `R2` | 330 Ω | One resistor per LED: 26.7 mA at 12 V. |
| `LED1`, `LED2` | White, 3.0 V, 7 Ω | A stand-in for the microscope's LEDs. |

Open on purpose: `PM1.outnod`, which gives the power as a voltage (1 V = 1 W).

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.31.0 (commit 6746f6a)
- Circuit: power_meter_dc.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

None reported.
</description>


